Tests
=====

None of these need the board.

C++ (firmware logic, run on the laptop with PlatformIO's native platform + Unity)
    pio test -e native                  all suites
    pio test -e native -f test_vm       one suite

    test_vm            expression VM: validator, every opcode, guarded maths, limits, and random programs
                       (valid ones always give a finite value; garbage is rejected or runs safely) (src/pattern/expr_vm.cpp)
    test_strip_config  LED strip length / direction clamping (src/led/strip_config.h)
    test_api_guard     API token comparison and the allow/refuse rule (src/net/api_guard_logic.h)
    test_audio         microphone maths: dB -> 0..1, band means, smoothing, tuning clamps (src/audio/audio_math.h)
    test_audio_packet  the 48-byte UDP audio packet from the PC server: layout, field order, NaN/huge values
                       are cleaned to 0..1 (src/audio/audio_packet.h)
    test_render        where each LED sits along its strip (0..1, reversed strips, one-LED strips) and how a
                       pattern value becomes a hue byte (src/led/render_math.h)
    test_audio_dsp     microphone signal processing: 90 Hz high-pass, Hann window, FFT, band levels, the
                       sample ring (src/audio/audio_dsp.h). Generated sine waves with known answers, no microphone.

    The logic under test lives in headers/files without Arduino or FreeRTOS includes, so it compiles
    on the laptop. The thin hardware wrappers (api_guard.cpp, the I2S capture) are not covered here.

    The native build uses -msse2 -mfpmath=sse so float maths is true 32-bit, like the ESP32. A 32-bit MinGW
    otherwise computes in 80-bit x87 registers and results differ from the board in the last bits.

JavaScript (web page and laptop server)
    npm install                         once: installs jsdom (a fake browser, development only)
    node test/js/run_all.js             everything below, files in parallel (also: npm test), about 12 s
    node test/js/test_server.js         one file

    test_compiler / test_differential   compiler.js, and its results against the firmware VM (needs g++)
    test_fuzz                           thousands of random programs, three ways: double-precision reference,
                                        browser preview, firmware VM. FUZZ_SEED=n FUZZ_N=20000 for more.
    test_firmware_sync                  opcode table, ABI, default pattern, program fingerprint, the UDP packet layout
                                        and ports (server.js against the firmware)
    test_presets / test_fastled         presets.js and fastled.js
    test_index_html                     structure of the page; slotHash() and presetsOnBoard()
    test_server                         server/server.js, started for real on free ports
    test_page_*                         the real index.html run in jsdom (see dom_harness.js):
                                          token  - API token wrapper, dialog, pairing link
                                          core   - tabs, idle sleep, Custom tab, preset upload, laptop mode
                                          tabs   - Patterns, Basic (wheel, white), Home, power, brightness
                                          setup  - strips, mic tuning, Wi-Fi, diagnostics

    dom_harness.js replaces fetch, timers and canvas with fakes: every request is recorded and answered
    by a route table, and page.tick(ms) moves a virtual clock, so nothing waits in real time.
    Not covered: real drawing and layout (jsdom has neither); that needs a real browser.

On the real board (needs the board on Wi-Fi; USB only for the reset steps)
    VIZ_PORT=COM3 node test/hardware/test_board.js
    Not part of run_all.js. Checks the API guard on the real firmware (reads open, POSTs refused without /
    with a wrong token, accepted with the right one) and NVS saving of the strip and mic settings (live
    changes are lost on reboot, saved ones survive, reset forgets them, bad input is refused, values are
    clamped). The token is read from include/secrets.h and never printed. Your strip and tuning settings are
    put back at the end, saved copy included, and compared with the start. Takes about 2 minutes.

    node test/hardware/test_board_api.js
    The HTTP API beyond the guard: /api/pattern (everything it must refuse and what it accepts, 64 ops, a stack 16
    deep, name handling, the program fingerprint against the web page's), /api/select, /api/power, /api/brightness,
    /api/white (the colour the board reports against an independent calculation), and the safe parts of the Wi-Fi API
    (status, scan, input validation). The lights DO change while it runs; it records the active pattern, power,
    brightness and white first and puts them back, then checks a valid program is rendering. Patterns go only to one
    spare slot, which keeps a small "hw_test" program afterwards (the API cannot delete a slot). It never sends a valid
    Wi-Fi network and never calls /api/wifi/forget with the token. Takes about a minute.

    The UDP audio path needs the board built with the UDP audio source, so it is a separate script:
      PLATFORMIO_BUILD_FLAGS=-DAUDIO_USE_UDP pio run -e esp32-s3-devkitc-1-n32r16v -t upload
      node test/hardware/test_udp_audio.js        (starts server/server.js, compares it with the board, then sends
                                                   malformed packets; stop any dev server on this laptop first)
      pio run -e esp32-s3-devkitc-1-n32r16v -t upload        (back to the microphone build)
    With the normal build it reports SKIPPED. test/hardware/mic_levels.js prints the microphone levels.

Automatic runs
    .github/workflows/tests.yml runs the native tests, the JavaScript tests and a firmware build on every push
    to main and every pull request. The on-board test above is not part of it (it needs the board).
